course: dictionary
lesson: 4931
title: Imperial: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Imperial has more than one meaning, so the words around it tell you which one you need. This is context, and it is your best clue.
explain: «The last imperial dynasty, the Qing, ruled from 1644 to 1912.» A dynasty is a family of rulers, so imperial means to do with an empire or an emperor here. Try it: «the last ruling family of an empire» fits the sentence.
explain: «Imperial units» are different. They are the old British measures, such as inches, feet, miles and pints. Here imperial is a name for a measuring system, not a ruler.
explain: «Imperial eagles» are birds. Imperial is part of the bird's name, so it does not mean empire at all. When a word sits inside a name, read the whole phrase before you decide.
fact: Imperial means to do with an empire or an emperor, as in «the imperial palace».
fact: Imperial units are the old British system of measures: inches, feet, miles, pints, pounds and stones.
fact: In the UK, 1 inch is about 2.5 cm, 1 mile is about 1.6 km, and 1 pint is about 568 ml.
fact: (formal) Imperial can also mean grand and commanding, like a powerful ruler.
fact: Useful phrases: «imperial system», «Imperial Rome», «imperial palace».
exercise: «The imperial palace stood at the heart of the empire.» What does imperial mean here? | Very small and cosy | * To do with an emperor or an empire | A type of measuring tape | Tired and hungry
exercise: Which of these is an imperial unit? | * Pint | Gram | Litre | Kilometre
exercise: «Imperial eagles» are a kind of bird. Does imperial mean empire in this phrase? | No, it means very small | * No, it is part of the bird's name | Yes, it means very old | Yes, the eagles ruled a country
test: In «imperial units», what does imperial mean? | Grand and commanding | * A system of old British measures like inches and pints | Belonging to an emperor's family | Very cold and icy
test: Which sentence uses imperial to mean grand and commanding? | «We measured the desk in feet and inches.» | * «He walked into the hall with an imperial air.» | «The last imperial dynasty ruled from 1644.» | «The imperial eagle hunts sousliks.»
exam: Why does the phrase «imperial eagles» tell you that imperial is not about an empire? | Because imperial is always a measure in a bird's name | Because you should always pick the first meaning you find | * Because the words around it show it is part of a bird's name | Because eagles only live in palaces
